# Categories

Choose exactly one category:

- `Actionable Target Gap`: legal input content, seed structure, API call sequence or target
  configuration can create the required state; the current fuzzers simply have not produced it.
  Normal result branches (e.g. an equality check returning false, a comparison of parsed values)
  usually belong here.
- `Bounded Extreme Value`: needs an extreme but legal and finite value or structure (maximum legal
  length, deep but finite nesting, a specific flag combination) that can be produced in practice.
- `Internal Invariant Guard`: defensive code for an internal state the program itself never creates
  (e.g. an `else` after all enum values are handled, an assertion-style sanity check on data the
  library already validated).
- `Structurally Unreachable API Path`: the current API path always fixes the condition to the
  non-blocked value and no legal alternative public API path or call sequence is apparent.
- `Resource-Exhaustion Guard`: the blocked side is an allocation or resource exhaustion failure.
- `Environmental Failure`: depends on system API failure, I/O failure or external environment state.
- `Generated Parser State`: Bison/Flex generated parser or scanner internal recovery state.
- `Infeasible Counter Overflow`: needs integer wraparound or impractically huge data structures.
- `Crash-Revealing Path`: the input would crash the target before the blocked side can be observed.
- `Uncertain`: the provided code is not enough to decide.

Rules:

- Trace where every value in the branch condition comes from before deciding. Only claim an input
  produces a value if you can see the data flow; do not assume an enum or field can take values
  that no code assigns.
- Prefer `Uncertain` over guessing. It does not penalize the blocker.
- Do not judge by how short the blocked side is; a one-line `return false;` can be a valuable
  semantic outcome, while a long block can be dead defensive code.

